00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:28.520
As a product manager, some
questions I often ask our users

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.600
are, are you running VMs in
more than one environment?

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.160
Are you operating in hybrid
or a multi-cloud environment?

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:41.420
What solutions are you using
for monitoring versus logging?

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.850
Do you have to leverage
multiple client-side agents

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.070
to capture telemetry?

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.650
And what tools are you
using to provision,

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.490
install, configure,
and manage your agents?

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:55.130
These questions usually
lead to one conclusion--

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:56.480
complexity.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.970
Simply put, users
have tons of VMs

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.790
that need to be instrumented
with one or more agents.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.690
These agents need to be
managed with one or more tools,

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.850
and even the
thought of migration

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.770
can be stress inducing.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.330
Let's quickly
visualize this to put

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:15.210
this problem in perspective.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.300
Often we think of only
our own environments.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.800
And despite this downscoping,
it still can be hectic.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.440
When we start to think about
all the permutations I just

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.140
discussed beyond
our own workloads

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.900
and think about this
issue at enterprise scale

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.650
with tens of thousands of
VMs, the problem of complexity

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:35.445
becomes glaring.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.740
For this reason, the integration
between Google Cloud and Cloud

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.320
Ops is a game changer
in reducing complexity,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.710
and why we at Cloud Ops
believe having an integrated

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.230
solution that meets
you where you are

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.050
is very important in helping
you manage and reduce

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.190
your ops burden.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:00.200
When we think about
simplification,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.810
we think about the agent.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.030
For this reason, we
embraced several principles

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.180
in making our latest
agent, the Ops Agent,

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.750
industry leading in its
user-friendly focus.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.490
Fewer agents-- this starts
right here at Google Cloud.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.500
Up until a couple
of months ago, we

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.010
had two agents, one for
logging and one for monitoring.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.430
We've invested heavily
in a single unified agent

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.680
to reduce the number of
agents and complexity

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.120
our users must manage.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.810
Number two, the ability to
manage our agents at scale

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.750
by investing in solutions
to easily support the most

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.740
popular config-management
tools that most of our users

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.550
are already leveraging.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.700
Simple configuration--
the Ops Agent

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.910
only has one
configuration file, making

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.480
it simple to manage both
logging and monitoring.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.750
Finally, third-party
application support--

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.730
here at Cloud Ops,
we're making the move

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.730
from monitoring the
platform to also monitoring

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.590
popular applications.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.360
We don't think
that requiring you

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:10.410
to install extra agents or extra
software to capture this data

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.750
is something that makes sense
or is a good use of your time.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:17.780
So we're building this
capability into the Ops Agent.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.490
One thing that's
not on this slide

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.610
and is crucial to
our strategy is

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:24.500
our investment in open source.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.500
We know the overall
story to telemetry

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:31.100
is not cutting it for
all operations users.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:35.540
And that includes our own users,
as well as everyone else's.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.360
Customers are simply
juggling too many agents,

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:43.600
too many protocols, and too
many ways to capture telemetry.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.900
In this vein, we decided to go
beyond a new agent and stake

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.750
out a path forward that would
make capturing telemetry

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.900
easier for all of our users.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:55.810
And we are doing this by
committing and supporting

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:59.160
open observability standards.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.230
The Ops Agent is
built to support

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.450
the future of
application and system

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:06.240
telemetry, which is
being democratized

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.890
through open source.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.520
We've incorporated two important
open-source technologies

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.230
into the Ops Agent.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.480
First, Fluent Bit.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.050
Fluent Bit is an open-source
log processor and forwarder,

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:22.470
which allows you to collect logs
with incredibly high throughput

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.030
and resource efficiency.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:28.440
The second technology
is OpenTelemetry.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.740
This is a CNCF-supported,
open-source,

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.770
and vendor-neutral technology
and is at the forefront

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:37.480
of unifying operations.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.570
It has garnered the support of
many vendors in the op space.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:44.800
And the Cloud Ops Agent
emphasizes our belief

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.080
that this technology will
lead to interoperability

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.340
within the ops community.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.880
We've worked very close with
the OpenTelemetry community.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.750
And through this effort, much
of which is frontier territory,

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:00.760
we are helping to guide
the greater telemetry

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.540
community to a place that is
optimized for you, the user.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.970
I mentioned these
topics earlier,

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:11.250
but I wanted to
specifically call out

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:15.240
how we are meeting our
users where they are.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.090
What I hear from
customers all the time is

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.820
that they are overwhelmed
with all of the tools

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.640
they have to manage
and learn to use.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.430
That's why we made sure that
the Ops Agent could be managed

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:29.610
by the open-source configuration
and provisioning tools

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:34.080
that you're already using, such
as Terraform, Ansible, Puppet,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.419
and Chef, and of course, our
in-house option, VM Manager.

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.640
In the demo, which
Ling will run shortly,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.440
you will see the
installation of the Ops

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.220
Agent handled by Ansible.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.050
Additionally, I
mentioned the support

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.270
for third-party applications.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:53.780
Currently we support
Nginx and JVM.

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.700
But we have an aggressive
rollout schedule

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:59.370
for the rest of this year
to include Apache, MySQL,

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.130
Redis, and many more.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.970
In 2022, we're going
to increase the support

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:09.500
by 50 additional third-party
applications directly built

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:10.580
into the Ops Agent.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.530
Finally, I want to review
our integration with GCP.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:21.640
While the Ops Agent is built
on OpenTelemetry and Fluent Bit

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:23.800
for the efficient
collection of telemetry,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:28.690
it is purpose built to support
our own operations suite,

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:31.630
which includes cloud
monitoring and logging.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:35.320
With the operations suite, we
want you to have reliability

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:37.480
without sacrificing agility.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.390
As you'll see in the demo, data
from cloud logging and cloud

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:44.380
monitoring is made available
directly in context

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.940
in Google Compute Engine.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:51.170
And many other GCP services
have similar integrations.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.250
Time for the demo.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.066
Ling, are you ready?

00:09:54.066 --> 00:09:55.920
LING HUANG: Ready for a demo.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.430
So in this demo, we're
going to show you

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:01.760
how to use the Google
Cloud Ops Agent Ansible

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:05.180
role to install the Ops
Agent on the fleet of VMs

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:10.010
