Ansible role is successful.

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:35.210
Yeah.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:39.410
I already installed this before,
so it's already installed,

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:42.320
as it shows.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:49.400
The examples playbook we are
using today looks like this.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:50.840
Looks pretty simple, right?

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.770
We are basically asking
Ansible to install the latest

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:59.900
version of Ops Agent with
a custom configuration file

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:04.820
on all the hosts that we
have in the inventory.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:09.230
Now, you might wonder, what is
in that ops-agent-config.yml?

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:11.840
Well, that's a good question.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.570
Basically, our Ops
Agent by default

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.660
collects some system
metrics and logs.

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:22.790
However, we can customize it to
collect more logs and metrics

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:24.560
from third-party applications.

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:28.130
And in this example, we
are collecting metrics

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.860
and logs from Nginx.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.950
On the metrics
side, I have already

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.380
pre-installed
Nginx on those VMs,

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:42.180
and their metrics expose via
their stub status module.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.690
So the key in this
configuration part

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:50.810
is to make sure that Ops Agent
knows basically which URL it

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:55.400
should be using to talk to their
stub status module of the Nginx

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.250
application to
collect the metrics.

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:00.350
And then, on the
logging side, we

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:06.890
collect both Nginx access
logs and Nginx error logs.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:12.110
OK, let's quit this, and
now it's the exciting part.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:15.410
Let's actually
execute the playbook.

